<% content_for :javascripts do %>
  <%= javascript_include_tag "api_docs", defer: true %>
<% end %>

<% content_for :description do %>
  A free JSON API with <%= number_with_delimiter @counts[:breeds] %> dog breeds,
  <%= @counts[:groups] %> breed groups, <%= number_with_delimiter @counts[:facts] %> dog facts,
  and <%= number_with_delimiter @counts[:images] %> pictures. No key, no sign up, nothing to pay.
<% end %>

<%= render "pages/hero", breeds: @hero_breeds if @hero_breeds.any? %>

<%# The heading says what the site is rather than repeating its name, which
    the header above it already carries. The dogs still open the page. %>
<%= page_heading "Dog breeds, groups, facts and pictures as a free JSON API",
      title: "Free JSON API for Dog Breeds, Pictures, Groups and Facts" %>

<p class="home-lead">
  Hi, I'm <%= link_to "kinduff", "https://kinduff.com", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener" %>.
  This is a free API with dog breeds, breed groups and facts. I've kept it
  running since 2016, mostly because people learning to code keep finding it
  useful. No key, no sign up, nothing to pay.
</p>

<% if @fact %>
  <blockquote class="home-fact">
    <%= @fact.body %>
    <cite>a fact this API served when you loaded the page</cite>
  </blockquote>
<% end %>

<p>
  Right now it holds <strong><%= number_with_delimiter @counts[:breeds] %></strong>
  breeds with their life spans and weights,
  <strong><%= number_with_delimiter @counts[:groups] %></strong> breed groups,
  <strong><%= number_with_delimiter @counts[:facts] %></strong> dog facts,
  and <strong><%= number_with_delimiter @counts[:images] %></strong> pictures of dogs.
  It answers about 60,000 requests on an average day, from universities,
  bootcamps, home labs and a lot of people poking at it on a Saturday.
</p>

<h2 id="start">
  <a class="api-anchor" href="#start" aria-hidden="true"></a>
  Try it
</h2>

<p>
  Nothing to install. Paste this into a terminal, or press the button and watch
  what comes back.
</p>

<%= render "pages/api_code",
      label: "Terminal",
      language: "shell",
      id: "home-curl",
      content: 'curl -s "https://dogapi.dog/api/v2/facts?limit=1"' %>

<form class="api-try" data-api-try data-base="/api/v2" data-path="/facts">
  <fieldset>
    <legend>Try it</legend>

    <div class="api-try-fields">
      <div class="api-try-field">
        <label for="home-limit">limit</label>
        <input type="text" id="home-limit" data-param="limit" data-in="query"
               data-default="1" value="1" placeholder="1-5">
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="api-try-actions">
      <button type="submit" class="api-button">Send request</button>
      <button type="button" class="api-button api-button-quiet" data-api-reset>Reset</button>
      <span class="api-muted">Runs against this site.</span>
    </div>
  </fieldset>

  <div class="api-try-output" data-api-output hidden>
    <figure class="code">
      <figcaption class="code-head">
        <span class="api-status" data-api-status></span>
        <code class="api-try-url" data-api-url></code>
      </figcaption>
      <pre class="code-body code-body-tall" data-api-body-box><code data-api-body></code></pre>
    </figure>
  </div>
</form>

<h2 id="facts">
  <a class="api-anchor" href="#facts" aria-hidden="true"></a>
  Dog facts API
</h2>

<p>
  That request is the facts endpoint. <code>/api/v2/facts</code> hands back a
  random dog fact each time, and <code>?limit=</code> asks for up to five at once.
  There are <%= number_with_delimiter @counts[:facts] %> of them, so repeats take a while.
</p>

<h2 id="images">
  <a class="api-anchor" href="#images" aria-hidden="true"></a>
  A dog image API in one tag
</h2>

<p>
  Pictures need even less than that. The image endpoint redirects straight to a
  file, so an <code>img</code> tag can point at it and nothing else is needed:
</p>

<%= render "pages/api_code",
      label: "A dog, in one tag",
      language: "html",
      id: "home-image-tag",
      content: %(<img src="https://dogapi.dog/api/v2/breeds/image">) %>

<p>
  No id means any dog. Add one — <code>/api/v2/breeds/&lt;id&gt;/image</code> —
  and you get that breed, which is where the pictures at the top of this page
  come from.
</p>

<p>
  When you want the rest, the <%= link_to "docs", docs_path %> explain every
  endpoint, and <%= link_to "the breeds pages", breeds_path %> let you browse all of them
  from the page.
</p>

<h2 id="teachers">
  <a class="api-anchor" href="#teachers" aria-hidden="true"></a>
  If you're teaching with it
</h2>

<p>
  Go ahead — course, workshop, tutorial, video, paid or free. You don't need to
  ask me and you don't need to credit me, though I always like hearing about it.
</p>

<p>
  A few things worth knowing before you put it in front of a class. There's no
  key, so nobody can leak one in a public repo or a screen share. It works
  straight from the browser, so <code>fetch()</code> in a static page or a
  CodePen is fine. The rate limit is 300 requests per minute per IP, which a
  whole room sharing one connection stays well under, and there's no monthly
  quota to run out of halfway through a lesson.
</p>

<p>
  If it breaks in the middle of a class, email me at
  <%= mail_to "dev@dogapi.dog" %> and I'll look at it.
</p>

<h3 id="exercises">
  <a class="api-anchor" href="#exercises" aria-hidden="true"></a>
  Things to build with it
</h3>

<p>
  In case you need somewhere to start: show a random fact and add a button that
  fetches another one. List every breed and filter them with a search box. Walk
  through the pages with <code>page[number]</code>. Pick a group and follow its
  breeds to fetch each one. Ask for a breed that doesn't exist and handle the
  404 without breaking the page. Chart the life spans across a group and argue
  about what the numbers actually mean.
</p>

<p>
  If you make something with it, send it over. Seeing what people build is the
  reason this is still online.
</p>

<p>
  There's more on this, with exercises by level and starter code you can paste
  into a lesson, in <%= link_to "Teaching with the Dog API", teach_path %>.
</p>

<%= image_tag "dogs.jpg",
      alt: "3 pugs with glasses in front of a computer in an office, digital art",
      width: 1280,
      height: 720,
      loading: "lazy",
      class: "home-image" %>

<h2 id="faq">
  <a class="api-anchor" href="#faq" aria-hidden="true"></a>
  Questions I get
</h2>

<%# Written once and used twice: rendered below, and handed to the structured
    data so a search engine quotes the same answer the page gives. %>
<% faqs = {} %>

<% faqs["Is the Dog API free?"] = capture do %>
  Yes. There's no paid plan and no monthly quota. It's mostly used for learning
  and for fun, and I've paid for it myself this long without it hurting. If you
  feel like helping with the bill, you can
  <%= link_to "buy me a coffee", "https://www.buymeacoffee.com/kinduff", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener" %>.
<% end %>

<% faqs["Do I need an API key?"] = capture do %>
  No, and I don't plan to require one. If that ever changes, whatever you've
  already built will keep working.
<% end %>

<% faqs["Does it work from the browser?"] = capture do %>
  Yes. The API sends CORS headers that allow any origin, so
  <code>fetch()</code> from a static page, a CodePen or a local file works
  without a proxy.
<% end %>

<% faqs["How do I get a random dog picture?"] = capture do %>
  Request <code>https://dogapi.dog/api/v2/breeds/image</code>. It redirects to a
  picture of a random dog, so you can use it as the <code>src</code> of an
  <code>img</code> tag directly.
<% end %>

<% faqs["What's the rate limit?"] = capture do %>
  <%= Rack::Attack::REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE %> requests per minute per IP. A whole
  classroom sharing one connection stays well under it.
<% end %>

<% faqs["Which version should I use?"] = capture do %>
  <%= link_to "v2", api_v2_path %>. I wrote v1 without following any standard
  and it doesn't grow well. v2 follows JSON:API and is where breeds, groups and
  anything new lives. <%= link_to "v1", api_v1_path %> still answers, and I'm
  not going to switch it off, but it's deprecated.
<% end %>

<% faqs["Where's the Slack button?"] = capture do %>
  I lost ownership of the Slack app to a policy change and couldn't get it back.
<% end %>

<% faqs["Is this the official dogapi.dog?"] = capture do %>
  Yes. It's mine,
  <%= link_to "kinduff", "https://kinduff.com", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener" %>,
  and I've run it since 2016. I like building silly things and putting them
  online. The code is
  <%= link_to "on GitHub", "https://github.com/kinduff/dogapi.dog", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener" %>,
  MIT licensed, and pull requests are welcome.
<% end %>

<% faqs.each do |question, answer| %>
  <h3><%= question %></h3>
  <p><%= answer %></p>
<% end %>

<% faq_structured_data(faqs) %>

